WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.206 --> 00:00:01.545
- With law school starting back up,

00:00:01.545 --> 00:00:03.298
I thought it be a good time to talk about

00:00:03.298 --> 00:00:07.212
the five biggest myths
about law school success,

00:00:07.212 --> 00:00:10.339
and how you can do better by working less.

00:00:10.339 --> 00:00:12.922
(gentle music)

00:00:14.956 --> 00:00:18.644
But first, let's define
what is law school success.

00:00:18.644 --> 00:00:22.014
For the most part law school
success is getting good grades

00:00:22.014 --> 00:00:24.433
to enable you to get the job that you want

00:00:24.433 --> 00:00:26.662
when you graduate from law school.

00:00:26.662 --> 00:00:28.445
Law school is a means to an end,

00:00:28.445 --> 00:00:30.867
and generally, as a professional school,

00:00:30.867 --> 00:00:33.738
is a means to the job
that you want to work

00:00:33.738 --> 00:00:36.116
as a career generally for
the rest of your life.

00:00:36.116 --> 00:00:39.395
And for most people, that
means working as a lawyer

00:00:39.395 --> 00:00:41.678
in the circumstances that they want.

00:00:41.678 --> 00:00:43.796
But it would be a pyrrhic victory

00:00:43.796 --> 00:00:47.617
to go through law school only
to lose more than you gain.

00:00:47.617 --> 00:00:49.915
For example, if you had a heart attack

00:00:49.915 --> 00:00:51.379
from all the stress that you were under,

00:00:51.379 --> 00:00:53.880
or lost your health because of all

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:55.952
the bad habits that you
gained in law school.

00:00:55.952 --> 00:00:56.842
So I wanna talk about

00:00:56.842 --> 00:00:58.947
some of the most pernicious myths

00:00:58.947 --> 00:01:00.952
about law school success.

00:01:00.952 --> 00:01:02.846
And the first one that
most people think of

00:01:02.846 --> 00:01:04.203
when they think of law school

00:01:04.203 --> 00:01:07.335
is that people work all the time.

00:01:07.335 --> 00:01:10.951
Now, certainly, law school is
going to take a lot of work,

00:01:10.951 --> 00:01:13.135
but no one can work all the time.

00:01:13.135 --> 00:01:15.665
No one can do all-nighter,
after all-nighter,

00:01:15.665 --> 00:01:17.305
after all-nighter.

00:01:17.305 --> 00:01:19.754
There has to be time for hobbies.

00:01:19.754 --> 00:01:21.250
One person once asked me

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:25.430
if they would have time for
their hobbies in law school.

00:01:25.430 --> 00:01:26.553
And my response was that

00:01:26.553 --> 00:01:29.746
not only is it possible to keep
your hobbies in law school,

00:01:29.746 --> 00:01:31.115
but I think it's essential.

00:01:31.115 --> 00:01:33.749
Law school is like a gas that expands

00:01:33.749 --> 00:01:36.054
to fill the space around it.

00:01:36.054 --> 00:01:37.865
Law school will take up all of the time

00:01:37.865 --> 00:01:39.154
that you allow it take up.

00:01:39.154 --> 00:01:40.918
And of course a lot of that is necessary,

00:01:40.918 --> 00:01:42.083
don't get me wrong.

00:01:42.083 --> 00:01:45.752
But every has to have a
life outside of law school.

00:01:45.752 --> 00:01:47.022
Everybody has to have hobbies

00:01:47.022 --> 00:01:48.538
and things they do on the side.

00:01:48.538 --> 00:01:51.028
So what I recommend is
paying yourself first.

00:01:51.028 --> 00:01:52.485
If your health is important to you,

00:01:52.485 --> 00:01:53.670
work out in the morning,

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:57.007
and let law school fill the
available time after that.

00:01:57.007 --> 00:01:58.937
If learning to play the
piano is important to you,

00:01:58.937 --> 00:02:02.099
carve out an hour of
the day to learn piano

00:02:02.099 --> 00:02:04.209
before you do all of your law school work.

00:02:04.209 --> 00:02:09.057
And on top of that, when you
focus on the high value work,

00:02:09.057 --> 00:02:10.899
you'll have a reasonable amount of time

00:02:10.899 --> 00:02:12.434
left over for other things.

00:02:12.434 --> 00:02:14.239
Those people that focus on the busy work,

00:02:14.239 --> 00:02:16.205
the case briefing, for example,

00:02:16.205 --> 00:02:18.824
they might not have all
that much extra time,

00:02:18.824 --> 00:02:19.888
because they're doing things

00:02:19.888 --> 00:02:21.956
that aren't going to help
them in the final exam.

00:02:21.956 --> 00:02:24.648
But if you focus on the high value work,

00:02:24.648 --> 00:02:27.830
like synthesizing the
law into your outline,

00:02:27.830 --> 00:02:29.537
doing practice exams,

00:02:29.537 --> 00:02:31.504
learning the strategies
for testing taking,

00:02:31.504 --> 00:02:33.240
you'll have a lot of time left over.

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:35.606
Focus on what's most important

00:02:35.606 --> 00:02:37.844
and don't work all the time.

00:02:37.844 --> 00:02:40.906
That brings me to the second
myth about law school success,

00:02:40.906 --> 00:02:42.926
which is that the smartest people

00:02:42.926 --> 00:02:44.981
will always get the best grades.

00:02:44.981 --> 00:02:46.309
What's funny is that

00:02:46.309 --> 00:02:48.179
it's clear when law school starts

00:02:48.179 --> 00:02:50.989
that some people are
extraordinarily smart,

00:02:50.989 --> 00:02:53.432
and some people are smarter then others.

00:02:53.432 --> 00:02:56.399
But the funny thing is
that those smartest people

00:02:56.399 --> 00:02:59.230
are rarely the people
that get the top grades.

00:02:59.230 --> 00:03:02.236
Just because you're smart
in an abstract sense,

00:03:02.236 --> 00:03:03.553
in an IQ sense,

00:03:03.553 --> 00:03:04.866
it doesn't mean that you're going to focus

00:03:04.866 --> 00:03:06.455
on the most important things.

00:03:06.455 --> 00:03:10.412
And so many people focus
on studying the law alone,

00:03:10.412 --> 00:03:14.369
without focusing on how
to take law school exams,

00:03:14.369 --> 00:03:15.601
which is an equally important part.

00:03:15.601 --> 00:03:19.126
In fact, we did an entire video
on the two keys to success.

00:03:19.126 --> 00:03:21.424
One is learning law itself,

00:03:21.424 --> 00:03:23.694
and the other is mastering the test.

00:03:23.694 --> 00:03:26.051
And if you do one at the
exclusion of the other,

00:03:26.051 --> 00:03:27.816
you're probably not gonna do as well.

00:03:27.816 --> 00:03:29.777
So just because you are smart

00:03:29.777 --> 00:03:32.490
in the sense that you can
memorize things faster

00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:36.123
or have a high IQ does not
guarantee success in law school.

00:03:36.123 --> 00:03:38.532
The third myth is along the same lines,

00:03:38.532 --> 00:03:41.998
and it's that everything in
law school is about hard work.

00:03:41.998 --> 00:03:44.934
The hardest workers will
be the most successful.

00:03:44.934 --> 00:03:47.136
Just as with the myth about

00:03:47.136 --> 00:03:49.048
the smartest people in law school,

00:03:49.048 --> 00:03:50.183
just because you work hard,

00:03:50.183 --> 00:03:52.944
it doesn't mean that you're
doing work that's important.

00:03:52.944 --> 00:03:55.413
There's so much bad
advice about law school,

00:03:55.413 --> 00:03:56.848
advice that will cause people to do

00:03:56.848 --> 00:03:59.349
far more busy work than is necessary.

00:03:59.349 --> 00:04:02.716
There are law school who in
their orientation packets,

00:04:02.716 --> 00:04:05.040
tell people that they should case brief

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:06.526
every case in law school,

00:04:06.526 --> 00:04:09.617
and that is just a huge waste of time.

00:04:09.617 --> 00:04:11.434
But because the law school

00:04:11.434 --> 00:04:13.700
is the one that's saying this information,

00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:16.274
it would be reasonable
for someone to think that

00:04:16.274 --> 00:04:18.272
they should do this work,

00:04:18.272 --> 00:04:20.682
and that if they're doing
a huge amount of work

00:04:20.682 --> 00:04:22.345
and killing themselves doing case brief

00:04:22.345 --> 00:04:24.625
night, after night, after night

00:04:24.625 --> 00:04:25.642
that they're getting ahead

00:04:25.642 --> 00:04:28.130
when really they're
just running in circles.

00:04:28.130 --> 00:04:29.691
So just because you're doing hard work

00:04:29.691 --> 00:04:31.446
doesn't mean you're doing the work

00:04:31.446 --> 00:04:35.986
that's going to result in a
successful law school career.

00:04:35.986 --> 00:04:38.943
Oh, and by the way,
feedback is the one thing

00:04:38.943 --> 00:04:41.717
that has been scientifically proven

00:04:41.717 --> 00:04:44.075
to increase your grades in law school.

00:04:44.075 --> 00:04:45.285
We did a whole video on that.

00:04:45.285 --> 00:04:47.059
Check it out in the link below.

00:04:47.059 --> 00:04:50.654
Hard work alone does not equal success.

00:04:50.654 --> 00:04:53.356
The finally myth about law school success

00:04:53.356 --> 00:04:56.070
is that grades are enough.

00:04:56.070 --> 00:04:58.429
Now certainly, grades are important,

00:04:58.429 --> 00:05:01.014
and they are probably
the most important thing

00:05:01.014 --> 00:05:03.304
that you should focus on in law school,

00:05:03.304 --> 00:05:05.053
but don't fool yourself to think that

00:05:05.053 --> 00:05:09.336
grades alone are enough to
get the job that you want.

00:05:09.336 --> 00:05:11.822
The legal field is still a field

00:05:11.822 --> 00:05:13.575
that relies on networks.

00:05:13.575 --> 00:05:15.698
It relies on who you know.

00:05:15.698 --> 00:05:19.181
So, regardless of what job
you want when you graduate,

00:05:19.181 --> 00:05:21.424
don't overlook the human element.

00:05:21.424 --> 00:05:23.397
Make friends with your professors.

00:05:23.397 --> 00:05:24.979
Go into office hours,

00:05:24.979 --> 00:05:27.571
make friends with other
attorneys in the field,

00:05:27.571 --> 00:05:30.032
other administrators in your law school,

00:05:30.032 --> 00:05:31.525
because these are the people

00:05:31.525 --> 00:05:33.279
that are going to be able to,

00:05:33.279 --> 00:05:36.209
for example, write letters
of recommendations.

00:05:36.209 --> 00:05:37.145
These are the people that might

00:05:37.145 --> 00:05:39.611
introduce you to other lawyers

00:05:39.611 --> 00:05:40.897
who you might want to work for.

00:05:40.897 --> 00:05:43.028
They might have connections to big firms.

00:05:43.028 --> 00:05:45.325
They might have connections to judges

00:05:45.325 --> 00:05:47.485
who you might want to clerk for.

00:05:47.485 --> 00:05:51.244
The human element cannot be overstates,

00:05:51.244 --> 00:05:54.633
and many law schools have the
on-campus interview process,

00:05:54.633 --> 00:05:59.113
and that's great, but not
everybody gets a job through OCI.

00:05:59.113 --> 00:06:01.914
You shouldn't put all of
your eggs in one basket,

00:06:01.914 --> 00:06:03.972
and you should still try and network

00:06:03.972 --> 00:06:05.811
even during your one-L year.

00:06:05.811 --> 00:06:08.954
It is much better to go to a professor

00:06:08.954 --> 00:06:11.797
whose office hours you went to many times,

00:06:11.797 --> 00:06:15.110
and to whom you've demonstrated

00:06:15.110 --> 00:06:17.180
a real understanding of the law

00:06:17.180 --> 00:06:19.906
and passion for the legal field

00:06:19.906 --> 00:06:23.498
to ask that professor for
a letter of recommendation

00:06:23.498 --> 00:06:25.369
after the semester is over.

00:06:25.369 --> 00:06:28.512
Remember, you're gonna have to apply for

00:06:28.512 --> 00:06:29.929
one-L summer jobs

00:06:31.149 --> 00:06:33.229
probably at the end of
your first semester,

00:06:33.229 --> 00:06:34.783
even before grades come out.

00:06:34.783 --> 00:06:37.814
And the only thing, at most, you'll have

00:06:37.814 --> 00:06:39.219
are your one-L grades.

00:06:39.219 --> 00:06:42.019
So you wanna be able to
talk to professors for

00:06:42.019 --> 00:06:43.143
letters of recommendation.

00:06:43.143 --> 00:06:45.562
You wanna be able to talk to other lawyers

00:06:45.562 --> 00:06:48.807
to perhaps have an
internship, or clerkship,

00:06:48.807 --> 00:06:50.807
or summer associateship.

00:06:51.748 --> 00:06:55.527
Don't overlook the fact that
you still have to network,

00:06:55.527 --> 00:06:57.896
and that grades are seldom enough

00:06:57.896 --> 00:07:00.102
to get your dream legal job.

00:07:00.102 --> 00:07:03.759
So, don't assume that your
grades are going to be enough.

00:07:03.759 --> 00:07:05.643
Maybe you're not gonna get the high grades

00:07:05.643 --> 00:07:08.427
that you think you should be entitled to.

00:07:08.427 --> 00:07:10.523
You may have to fall back
on that human element.

00:07:10.523 --> 00:07:13.986
And regardless, it's always
better to backstop grades

00:07:13.986 --> 00:07:15.611
with a personal network

00:07:15.611 --> 00:07:18.299
that you developed well in law school

00:07:18.299 --> 00:07:20.491
to make sure that you can attain

00:07:20.491 --> 00:07:23.571
the perfect legal job when you graduate.

00:07:23.571 --> 00:07:24.802
Hopefully that was helpful.

00:07:24.802 --> 00:07:27.051
If you like this video, please
click like and subscribe

00:07:27.051 --> 00:07:29.068
for more law school freshman tips.

00:07:29.068 --> 00:07:31.551
Also, please download
the free checklist below

00:07:31.551 --> 00:07:35.595
to ensure your successful
law school career.

00:07:35.595 --> 00:07:36.641
From everyone here at Legal Eagle,

00:07:36.641 --> 00:07:37.711
thanks for stopping by.

